<p>A dispute is growing around the Wireless Festival in London after organizers booked the American rapper Ye, formerly known as Kanye West, to headline the event this July. The backlash sharpened after U.K. Prime Minister Keir Starmer said he was “deeply concerned” about the decision, while critics pointed to Ye’s past antisemitic and racist remarks and, in one report, activities glorifying Nazis. So far, the clearest impact has been commercial: Pepsi and Diageo withdrew their sponsorships after Ye’s booking was announced. What began as a summer festival lineup decision has turned into a wider debate in Britain about public standards, corporate accountability and how promoters handle artists whose past statements have drawn condemnation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ZqFSp09U97tZanyo2IYtcFBtcNZR1wUsp7WS4nkJFzroK3Q4RvNCd-fgUFQMrSauD7ZESqDja5SzF1VHUCHgSR-qKWZ5MKTQnEM_HWaqWDdJg6vHLwajO5nweZVXyKvu_-W6-WVbF3lcn3JWm3oEoUCH0mOIgJFwzp7yeodl9mPn9t_YPrA4smuSicExgA' alt='Kanye West in Milan in 2024.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Sponsor exits: Pepsi, described as the festival’s top sponsor, and drinks company Diageo both pulled out after the lineup announcement.</li><li>Three-night set: Ye is scheduled to perform on three nights at Wireless Festival in July as part of what one report described as a European comeback tour.</li><li>Political scrutiny: Starmer’s criticism pushed the issue beyond an entertainment booking dispute and into national politics in the U.K..</li><li>Brand risk: The sponsor withdrawals show how quickly artist bookings can affect festival financing and partner relationships when reputational concerns arise.</li></ul><blockquote>Deeply concerned - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: The British prime minister said he was “deeply concerned” that Ye had been booked to perform at the London festival. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/05/world/europe/ye-music-festival-pepsi-antisemitism.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Pepsi: The company withdrew its sponsorship of Wireless Festival as the booking controversy intensified. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3349102/british-pm-slams-london-music-festival-booking-kanye-west-sponsor-pepsi-quits-event'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/pressure-grows-to-remove-ye-formerly-known-as-kanye-west-as-the-headliner-of-london-summer-music-festival'>Pressure grows to remove Ye, formerly known as Kanye West, as the headliner of London summer music festival</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/05/nx-s1-5774562/kanye-west-ye-wireless-festival-london-uk-pepsi-diageo'>A music festival booked Kanye West, now known as Ye, and lost major sponsors</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3349102/british-pm-slams-london-music-festival-booking-kanye-west-sponsor-pepsi-quits-event'>Major sponsors drop out of London festival headlined by Kanye West</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/05/world/europe/ye-music-festival-pepsi-antisemitism.html'>Pepsi Drops Sponsorship of Wireless Festival Headlined by Kanye West</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/pressure-mounts-ye-pulled-headline-role-summer-festival-131746533'>Pressure mounts on Ye to be pulled from his headline role at a summer festival in London</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>